<v DIGILORMO>Before the babies.

504
00:20:31.321 --> 00:20:31.714
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

505
00:20:31.714 --> 00:20:32.500
<v DIGILORMO>So alright.

506
00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:34.464
You were living in Duson.

507
00:20:34.464 --> 00:20:35.643
<v MONTGOMERY>Living in Duson.

508
00:20:35.643 --> 00:20:37.214
<v DIGILORMO>Your parents were farmers.

509
00:20:37.214 --> 00:20:38.000
<v MONTGOMERY>Yeah.

510
00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.205
<v DIGILORMO>Did you go to school in Duson?

511
00:20:40.205 --> 00:20:41.151
<v MONTGOMERY>Just one year.

512
00:20:41.151 --> 00:20:41.781
<v DIGILORMO>One year.

513
00:20:41.781 --> 00:20:42.726
<v MONTGOMERY>I’ve no education.

514
00:20:42.726 --> 00:20:43.041
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

515
00:20:43.041 --> 00:20:44.301
<v MONTGOMERY>I went one year.

516
00:20:44.301 --> 00:20:45.877
I never forget the book.

517
00:20:45.877 --> 00:20:48.082
The book was named Bob and Nancy.

518
00:20:48.082 --> 00:20:48.397
<v DIGILORMO>[laughs]

519
00:20:48.397 --> 00:20:50.603
<v MONTGOMERY>That was the name of the book.

520
00:20:50.603 --> 00:20:53.123
They draw them little pictures on the book.

521
00:20:53.123 --> 00:20:56.274
And I never wanted to go to school no more.

522
00:20:56.274 --> 00:20:58.795
I did not go to school no more.

523
00:20:58.795 --> 00:21:01.000
<v DIGILORMO>And your parents didn't make you?

524
00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:01.787
<v MONTGOMERY>No. They tried.

525
00:21:01.787 --> 00:21:04.672
I said . . . I told them no. I said, "No." So my

526
00:21:04.672 --> 00:21:06.770
<c.p>daddy said, "You have to go to work."

527
00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:08.607
I said, "Well I'll go to work."

528
00:21:08.607 --> 00:21:10.967
I went to work in a sweet potato field.

529
00:21:10.967 --> 00:21:12.279
Make forty cents an hour.

530
00:21:12.279 --> 00:21:12.541
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

531
00:21:12.541 --> 00:21:13.590
<v MONTGOMERY>Forty cent an hour.

532
00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:14.639
I went to work . . .

533
00:21:14.639 --> 00:21:15.426
<v DIGILORMO>Sweet potato field.

534
00:21:15.426 --> 00:21:17.000
<v MONTOGMERY>Willy Norman Sweet Potato Factory.

535
00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:17.368
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

536
00:21:17.368 --> 00:21:18.105
<v MONTGOMERY>In Scott.

537
00:21:18.105 --> 00:21:18.474
<v DIGILORMO>Louisiana.

538
00:21:18.474 --> 00:21:19.211
<v MONTGOMERY>Scott, Louisiana.

539
00:21:19.211 --> 00:21:20.316
<v DIGILORMO>Right outside Lafayette.

540
00:21:20.316 --> 00:21:21.789
<v MONTGOMERY>That was my job.

541
00:21:21.789 --> 00:21:27.684
I was born in Duson, but I went to the . . . About five miles over in Scott.

542
00:21:27.684 --> 00:21:30.263
The next five miles you in Lafayette.

543
00:21:30.263 --> 00:21:31.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

544
00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.300
<v MONTGOMERY>You know they're all together.

545
00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:36.520
You got Rayne, Duson, Scott, and Lafayette.

546
00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:40.660
<v DIGILORMO>So how long did you work as a potato . . .

547
00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.040
<v MONTGOMERY>I worked there . . .

548
00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:43.880
<v DIGILORMO>A sweet potato picker.

549
00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:50.320
<v MONTGOMERY>I worked there . . . I was twelve years old when I give my [ . . .?] Alberta Broussard.

550
00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:54.460
And then I went to work there at Taylor [?].

551
00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:57.220
I thought I was a man.

552
00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:01.360
I got this woman here I'm working the Taylor [?].

553
00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:03.660
Wes, you a man now.

554
00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:07.340
So I worked there until I was thirteen.

555
00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:11.020
When I made thirteen the work slowed up.

556
00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:16.080
So I went to cutting sugar cane in New Iberia [Louisiana].

557
00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:17.000
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

558
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.909
<v MONTGOMERY>Working with a bunch of men.

559
00:22:19.909 --> 00:22:23.788
The youngest one was about sixty years old.

560
00:22:23.788 --> 00:22:30.091
They was a bunch . . . I was sleeping in a camp with these guys.

561
00:22:30.091 --> 00:22:30.576
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

562
00:22:30.576 --> 00:22:33.970
<v MONTGOMERY>And I'm just thirteen, fourteen years old.

563
00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:35.909
I'm in the camp.

564
00:22:35.909 --> 00:22:42.212
But God, you see God was with me when I didn't even know.

565
00:22:42.212 --> 00:22:49.000
When we had dinner that woman would put us to eat in the house.

566
00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:54.818
I always wanted to eat milk, and cornbread, and couche [cornmeal mush].

567
00:22:54.818 --> 00:22:59.667
And she told me, "Look we got steaks and beef."

568
00:22:59.667 --> 00:23:05.970
No, give me some couche couche like my mama did and some cornbread.

569
00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:11.303
She says, "Son you . . ." I said, “No, that's all I need.

570
00:23:11.303 --> 00:23:14.212
One day she looked at me.

571
00:23:14.212 --> 00:23:16.636
She said, "What your name?"

572
00:23:16.636 --> 00:23:17.121
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>Hey [?].

573
00:23:17.121 --> 00:23:18.091
<v MONTGOMERY>Yes, baby.

574
00:23:18.091 --> 00:23:21.000
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>Hey, how are you doing?

575
00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:21.461
<v DIGILORMO>Hi!

576
00:23:21.461 --> 00:23:23.304
Good, how are you?

577
00:23:23.304 --> 00:23:24.687
<v MONTGOMERY>Where we was?

578
00:23:24.687 --> 00:23:29.756
<v DIGILORMO>Well you were telling me a bit about your mother's cooking.

579
00:23:29.756 --> 00:23:33.442
I was kind of curious about that, too.

580
00:23:33.442 --> 00:23:37.590
What did you grow up eating a lot of?

581
00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:41.737
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, yeah, well I was working in New Iberia.

582
00:23:41.737 --> 00:23:43.120
<v DIGILORMO>Oh okay, yeah.

583
00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:46.806
<v MONTGOMERY>So the woman come to me one day.

584
00:23:46.806 --> 00:23:49.111
It was all about Jesus.

585
00:23:49.111 --> 00:23:50.954
She looked at me.

586
00:23:50.954 --> 00:23:57.406
You know, she thought I was a good looking man and looked at me.

587
00:23:57.406 --> 00:23:59.249
Said, "What your name?"

588
00:23:59.249 --> 00:24:02.475
I told her I said, "Wesley Montgomery."

589
00:24:02.475 --> 00:24:05.240
Said, "What's your daddy name was?"

590
00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:07.083
I said, "Abraham Montgomery."

591
00:24:07.083 --> 00:24:10.770
She says, "Was your mother named Mary Montgomery?"

592
00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:12.613
I said, "Yes ma'am."

593
00:24:12.613 --> 00:24:14.917
Then she began to cry.

594
00:24:14.917 --> 00:24:19.065
She says, "You been sleeping with them grown men.

595
00:24:19.065 --> 00:24:21.369
Them fifty, sixty years old."

596
00:24:21.369 --> 00:24:23.212
They had a camp.

597
00:24:23.212 --> 00:24:24.594
A big camp.

598
00:24:24.594 --> 00:24:28.742
In the wintertime it was cold with no heaters.

599
00:24:28.742 --> 00:24:31.968
And I'm only thirteen, fourteen years old.

600
00:24:31.968 --> 00:24:36.115
She said, "I don't want you out there anymore."

601
00:24:36.115 --> 00:24:39.802
And I didn't want to lose my job.

602
00:24:39.802 --> 00:24:42.567
I didn't know what she meant.

603
00:24:42.567 --> 00:24:48.097
She said, "From now on you going to sleep in the house."

604
00:24:48.097 --> 00:24:50.401
Said, "I got three sons."

605
00:24:50.401 --> 00:24:54.548
Ernie, Darrell, and the other name I can't remember.

606
00:24:54.548 --> 00:24:56.853
Darrell, he passed for white.

607
00:24:56.853 --> 00:25:00.078
Just like me . . . he passed for white.

608
00:25:00.078 --> 00:25:01.000
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

609
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:02.267
<v MONTGOMERY>Then Ernest Junior.

610
00:25:02.267 --> 00:25:07.758
She said, "You and Ernest Junior you all going to share a bed.

611
00:25:07.758 --> 00:25:11.559
You going to sleep together in the same bed.

612
00:25:11.559 --> 00:25:16.627
And Darrell and the other boy they going to share that bed."

613
00:25:16.627 --> 00:25:20.429
I says, "I'm going to stay in the house?"

614
00:25:20.429 --> 00:25:21.696
She says, "Yes."

615
00:25:21.696 --> 00:25:25.075
Say, "Me and your mother is close kin."

616
00:25:25.075 --> 00:25:26.342
I said, "What?"

617
00:25:26.342 --> 00:25:28.876
She says, "Me and your mother.

618
00:25:28.876 --> 00:25:30.565
We is close relatives."

619
00:25:30.565 --> 00:25:31.410
DIGILORMO: Wow.

620
00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:32.677
<v MONTGOMERY>I told her.

621
00:25:32.677 --> 00:25:36.056
I say, "You is kin to my mother?"

622
00:25:36.056 --> 00:25:39.012
She say, "Oh yes close, close relatives."

623
00:25:39.012 --> 00:25:42.814
She said, "I'm going to take care of you."

624
00:25:42.814 --> 00:25:46.193
So we started going out on the weekends.

625
00:25:46.193 --> 00:25:48.304
Me and them three boys.

626
00:25:48.304 --> 00:25:50.416
Well one of them he

627
00:25:50.416 --> 00:25:52.528
<c.p>was, he passed for white.

628
00:25:52.528 --> 00:25:52.950
Darrell.

629
00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.484
He looked like he was white.

630
00:25:55.484 --> 00:25:56.329
You know.

631
00:25:56.329 --> 00:25:59.708
See black folks, they not like you all.

632
00:25:59.708 --> 00:26:03.087
We got . . . Like in my family same thing.

633
00:26:03.087 --> 00:26:06.466
The other two brothers they were like me.

634
00:26:06.466 --> 00:26:09.000
But Darrell, yeah he . . . Shit.

635
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.472
So we go to them [carton?] dance in the country.

636
00:26:12.472 --> 00:26:18.028
We'd drop him off to the white accordion dance and we'd go to the black one.

637
00:26:18.028 --> 00:26:19.417
The other two brothers.

638
00:26:19.417 --> 00:26:25.667
We'd come back and pick him up about twelve o’clock, twelve-thirty pick him up to the white place.

639
00:26:25.667 --> 00:26:28.097
We'd come on back to the house.

640
00:26:28.097 --> 00:26:29.833
We’d do that every weekend.

641
00:26:29.833 --> 00:26:31.917
So everybody thought he was white.

642
00:26:31.917 --> 00:26:33.306
But he was black.

643
00:26:33.306 --> 00:26:37.472
So that went on until . . . I work there a couple of years.

644
00:26:37.472 --> 00:26:41.292
Till I moved to . . . That's when I moved to Lake Charles.

645
00:26:41.292 --> 00:26:46.847
When I lived there I came on to Lake Charles and I moved with my brother.

646
00:26:46.847 --> 00:26:47.194
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

647
00:26:47.194 --> 00:26:47.889
<v MONTGOMERY>And then . . .

648
00:26:47.889 --> 00:26:51.014
<v DIGILORMO>And when you say Lake Charles, you mean Mossville?

649
00:26:51.014 --> 00:26:53.097
<v MONTGOMERY>I mean . . . Nothing about Lake Charles.

650
00:26:53.097 --> 00:26:55.181
I don't know nothing about here.

651
00:26:55.181 --> 00:26:56.222
Was all Mossville.

652
00:26:56.222 --> 00:26:59.000
<v DIGILORMO>So what drew your brother to Mossville?

653
00:26:59.000 --> 00:26:59.399
<v MONTGOMERY>[Pardon]?

654
00:26:59.399 --> 00:27:02.191
<v DIGILORMO>What made your brother move to Mossville?

655
00:27:02.191 --> 00:27:06.181
<v MONTGOMERY>He left from Duson, Scott, to get a job there.

656
00:27:06.181 --> 00:27:08.574
There were more jobs over there.

657
00:27:08.574 --> 00:27:09.771
He move here.

658
00:27:09.771 --> 00:27:14.160
And we couldn't get no job here, it was just bad.

659
00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:14.559
<v DIGILORMO>Really?

660
00:27:14.559 --> 00:27:16.553
<v MONTGOMERY>So what the peeling pole.

661
00:27:16.553 --> 00:27:18.947
Telephone pole . . . Went to peel it.

662
00:27:18.947 --> 00:27:20.144
Cutting trees down.

663
00:27:20.144 --> 00:27:25.330
The same thing we'd peel the poles, and they'd soak them in creosote.

664
00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:29.319
And they . . . That's where you got all your electric from.

665
00:27:29.319 --> 00:27:34.505
So we went into that because there was nothing else you could do.

666
00:27:34.505 --> 00:27:37.298
<v DIGILORMO>Now . . . so did . . . You lived in Mossville?

667
00:27:37.298 --> 00:27:39.293
Did you work in Mossville?

668
00:27:39.293 --> 00:27:41.287
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, I stayed in Mossville.

669
00:27:41.287 --> 00:27:42.883
Mossville was my home.

670
00:27:42.883 --> 00:27:44.878
<v DIGILORMO>So the telephone pole job.

671
00:27:44.878 --> 00:27:46.473
That was in Mossville?

672
00:27:46.473 --> 00:27:48.069
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh yes, in Mossville.

673
00:27:48.069 --> 00:27:50.064
We'd peel all the poles.

674
00:27:50.064 --> 00:27:50.862
Poke wood.

675
00:27:50.862 --> 00:27:51.261
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

676
00:27:51.261 --> 00:27:54.053
<v MONTGOMERY>We did all of that in Mossville.

677
00:27:54.053 --> 00:27:58.043
<v DIGILORMO>What year was . . . What year about was that you think?

678
00:27:58.043 --> 00:27:59.638
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh, Lord have mercy.

679
00:27:59.638 --> 00:28:01.234
<v DIGILORMO>You were how old?

680
00:28:01.234 --> 00:28:05.223
<v MONTGOMERY>When I come to Mossville I was fourteen years old.

681
00:28:05.223 --> 00:28:06.819
When I moved there.

682
00:28:06.819 --> 00:28:08.814
<v DIGILORMO>So that'd have been 1948.

683
00:28:08.814 --> 00:28:10.410
<v MONTGOMERY>1948 that's about right.

684
00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:14.000
<v DIGILORMO>So what did Mossville look like in 1948?

685
00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:15.333
<v MONTGOMERY>They had nothing.

686
00:28:15.333 --> 00:28:16.667
There was wood.

687
00:28:16.667 --> 00:28:17.556
<v DIGILORMO>Just wood?

688
00:28:17.556 --> 00:28:18.889
<v MONTGOMERY>Nothing but wood.

689
00:28:18.889 --> 00:28:23.333
All they had there was the Lemelle and the Hartman.

690
00:28:23.333 --> 00:28:23.778
<v DIGILORMO>Hartman?

691
00:28:23.778 --> 00:28:25.111
<v MONTGOMERY>Hartman and Lemelle.

692
00:28:25.111 --> 00:28:25.556
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

693
00:28:25.556 --> 00:28:27.778
<v MONTGOMERY>There was nothing but wood.

694
00:28:27.778 --> 00:28:29.111
Mossville wasn't nothing.

695
00:28:29.111 --> 00:28:29.556
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

696
00:28:29.556 --> 00:28:34.000
So how many people do you think lived there?

697
00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:44.556
<v MONTGOMERY>Oh all together . . . I'd say everything in Mossville was maybe twenty-five . . . Twenty to twenty-five people.

698
00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:45.259
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

699
00:28:45.259 --> 00:28:48.074
<v MONTGOMERY>There was nobody there.

700
00:28:48.074 --> 00:28:51.593
Then we move in there.

701
00:28:51.593 --> 00:28:57.222
Then everybody started moving in from the country.

702
00:28:57.222 --> 00:29:00.741
Lafayette, Carencro, Sunset, Duson, Scott.

703
00:29:00.741 --> 00:29:03.556
Moving in to Mossville.

704
00:29:03.556 --> 00:29:09.889
<v DIGILORMO>So you saw an influx of people moving in?

705
00:29:09.889 --> 00:29:10.593
<v MONTGOMERY>Yes.

706
00:29:10.593 --> 00:29:12.000
<v DIGILORMO>Wow.

707
00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:24.412
<v MONTGOMERY>And when I came back from Los Angeles . . .when I ran away from home . . . when I came back here . . . Well I thought I was a tremendous man then.

708
00:29:24.412 --> 00:29:28.402
So I'm young, good-looking, so I got into politics.

709
00:29:28.402 --> 00:29:28.845
<v DIGILORMO>Okay.

710
00:29:28.845 --> 00:29:29.732
<v MONTGOMERY>Heavy, strong.

711
00:29:29.732 --> 00:29:34.608
I got in . . . I was . . . I got so big into politics . . .

712
00:29:34.608 --> 00:29:35.495
<v DIGILORMO>How so?

713
00:29:35.495 --> 00:29:35.938
<v MONTGOMERY>[Pardon]?

714
00:29:35.938 --> 00:29:39.041
<v DIGILORMO>How did you get in to politics?

715
00:29:39.041 --> 00:29:41.258
Like what did you do?

716
00:29:41.258 --> 00:29:42.588
Or did you . . .

717
00:29:42.588 --> 00:29:47.021
<v MONTGOMERY>The way it started, my wife got in a wreck.

718
00:29:47.021 --> 00:29:52.784
My first wife got in a wreck on Highway 90 and Prater Road.

719
00:29:52.784 --> 00:29:54.113
<v DIGILORMO>On Prater Road.

720
00:29:54.113 --> 00:29:55.000
Okay.

721
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.627
<v MONTGOMERY>And the state . . . She was . . . She was right.

722
00:29:58.627 --> 00:30:01.348
She run into a white girl.

723
00:30:01.348 --> 00:30:06.335
And my wife was right, and the white girl was wrong.

724
00:30:06.335 --> 00:30:11.776
I was then twenty-two years old then . . . I was a man then.

725
00:30:11.776 --> 00:30:12.230
Twenty-two.

726
00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:13.590
I was married.

727
00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:20.391
So when the state troopers come, they passed my wife up like she wasn't nobody.

728
00:30:20.391 --> 00:30:20.845
<v DIGILORMO>Yeah.

729
00:30:20.845 --> 00:30:23.112
<v MONTGOMERY>Didn't even talk to her.

730
00:30:23.112 --> 00:30:25.832
They went to the white girl.

731
00:30:25.832 --> 00:30:28.099
That’s the way it was

732
00:30:28.099 --> 00:30:29.460
<c.p>in those days.

733
00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:30.820
Give it here.

734
00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:34.901
Honey, that's the way it was in those days.

735
00:30:34.901 --> 00:30:36.714
<v DIGILORMO>Hey, I understand that.

736
00:30:36.714 --> 00:30:41.248
<v MONTGOMERY>So my . . . So he went on and I got pissed.

737
00:30:41.248 --> 00:30:44.422
What I had told that state trooper.

738
00:30:44.422 --> 00:30:47.596
See, I was building my first house.

739
00:30:47.596 --> 00:30:51.677
So I want everything both sides of the street.

740
00:30:51.677 --> 00:30:53.037
All these houses?

741
00:30:53.037 --> 00:30:54.851
That's all for me.

742
00:30:54.851 --> 00:30:59.839
Here all of this, that beauty salon . . . That’s all my stuff.

743
00:30:59.839 --> 00:31:01.199
It's all Jesus.

744
